1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,347
Five minutes past midnight, the thirty-first of March, nineteen ninety.

2
00:00:05,347 --> 00:00:14,599
Two F-16 fighter jets leave a Belgian air base, chasing triangles of light that the Air Force itself will never be able to explain.

3
00:00:15,066 --> 00:00:16,126
Radar saw them.

4
00:00:16,126 --> 00:00:17,257
Police saw them.

5
00:00:17,257 --> 00:00:20,648
Thousands of ordinary people said they saw them.

6
00:00:20,648 --> 00:00:25,100
And the pilots, with every sensor on board, saw nothing at all.

7
00:00:25,566 --> 00:00:29,629
So was this the best-documented alien visit in history?

8
00:00:29,629 --> 00:00:34,799
Or the best-documented example of how a whole country can fool itself?

9
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:38,233
Tonight we run it like the Air Force did.

10
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:38,884
An alert.

11
00:00:38,884 --> 00:00:39,535
A launch.

12
00:00:39,535 --> 00:00:40,476
An intercept.

13
00:00:40,476 --> 00:00:44,166
And then a debrief, where every claim gets a score.

14
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:49,237
Facts first, theories labelled, and no triangle gets a free pass.

15
00:00:49,237 --> 00:00:50,299
Alert received.

16
00:00:54,766 --> 00:00:55,635
Chapter one.

17
00:00:55,635 --> 00:00:56,359
The alert.

18
00:00:56,359 --> 00:01:05,700
It begins on the twenty-ninth of November, nineteen eighty-nine, in Eupen, a town in the east of Belgium, near the German border.

19
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:09,826
Two gendarmes on patrol see something in the sky.

20
00:01:09,826 --> 00:01:13,935
A large object, low, with bright lights, moving slowly.

21
00:01:13,935 --> 00:01:15,055
They report it.

22
00:01:15,055 --> 00:01:18,566
A local German-language paper prints the story.

23
00:01:19,033 --> 00:01:20,607
Then the story spreads.

24
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:21,634
Reporters call.

25
00:01:21,634 --> 00:01:22,934
Experts are quoted.

26
00:01:22,934 --> 00:01:29,299
And after more than a week of coverage, a hundred and forty-three people say they saw it too.

27
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:36,587
Experts, here, means a group called SOBEPS, the Belgian Society for the Study of Space Phenomena.

28
00:01:36,587 --> 00:01:40,666
Its leading voice is a physics professor, Auguste Meessen.

29
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:51,000
The picture that spreads is a black triangle, perhaps two hundred feet wide, with three lights underneath, drifting almost silently over the rooftops.

30
00:01:51,466 --> 00:01:54,662
The sceptics read the first night differently.

31
00:01:54,662 --> 00:02:01,054
The gendarmes reported a low engine noise, and one witness described a stick with a turbine.

32
00:02:01,054 --> 00:02:03,833
That sounds like a Bell Huey helicopter.

33
00:02:04,299 --> 00:02:12,425
And most of their report concerned a steady white light above a lake, exactly where the planet Venus would have been.

34
00:02:12,425 --> 00:02:15,966
Real people, honest reports, ordinary explanations.

35
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:18,178
But reports keep arriving.

36
00:02:18,178 --> 00:02:23,482
Through the winter of nineteen eighty-nine, they come in from all over Belgium.

37
00:02:23,482 --> 00:02:25,900
The Air Force begins to take notice.

38
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:31,193
By December the Belgian Air Force is receiving witness calls itself.

39
00:02:31,193 --> 00:02:34,316
Triangles, lights beaming down, a faint hum.

40
00:02:34,316 --> 00:02:40,633
It sets a rule: only scramble if police confirm the lights, and radar sees something too.

41
00:02:41,099 --> 00:02:41,927
Chapter two.

42
00:02:41,927 --> 00:02:42,685
The ground.

43
00:02:42,685 --> 00:02:50,199
The night of the thirtieth of March, nineteen ninety, a clear, quiet Friday evening over the Walloon Brabant.

44
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:55,973
At eleven p.m., a gendarmerie officer phones the radar centre at Glons.

45
00:02:55,973 --> 00:03:00,906
From his home at Ramillies, he sees three lights above the fields.

46
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:05,166
Brighter than stars, changing colour: red, green, yellow.

47
00:03:05,633 --> 00:03:07,744
They form a perfect triangle.

48
00:03:07,744 --> 00:03:09,272
And they do not move.

49
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:16,333
A controller does what the rules say: he asks the Wavre gendarmerie to send a patrol and confirm.

50
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,340
At about twenty-three thirty, the patrol arrives.

51
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:27,709
Captain Pinson describes lights the size of a large star, changing from red to blue to green to white.

52
00:03:27,709 --> 00:03:30,599
Clear, he says, as if they were signals.

53
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:39,074
Then three more lights appear and drift toward the first, after some disordered movements settling into a second triangle.

54
00:03:39,074 --> 00:03:41,699
Seen by several people, from the ground.

55
00:03:42,166 --> 00:03:49,156
Gendarmes from four other stations, each about ten miles apart, will later say they saw the lights too.

56
00:03:49,156 --> 00:03:50,038
That matters.

57
00:03:50,038 --> 00:03:55,399
Independent police witnesses are the strongest kind of ground evidence we have.

58
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:59,688
But notice what ground witnesses can and cannot give us.

59
00:03:59,688 --> 00:04:02,555
Colour, brightness, and a rough direction.

60
00:04:02,555 --> 00:04:04,330
They cannot give distance.

61
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:08,766
A bright light with no landmark can be a mile away, or a hundred.

62
00:04:09,233 --> 00:04:14,170
Even a bright star low on the horizon flashes red, green and white.

63
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,496
Hold that thought.

64
00:04:15,496 --> 00:04:19,401
Because tonight, the lights are not the only witness.

65
00:04:19,401 --> 00:04:20,433
Over to radar.

66
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:21,912
Chapter three.

67
00:04:21,912 --> 00:04:22,345
Radar.

68
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:31,600
The Glons Control and Reporting Centre watches the sky over central Belgium, and tonight its controller finds something unusual.

69
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:36,674
About eight nautical miles north of the sighting, a slow contact.

70
00:04:36,674 --> 00:04:41,565
Roughly twenty-five knots, heading west, at around ten thousand feet.

71
00:04:41,565 --> 00:04:43,833
Slower than a car on a motorway.

72
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:51,570
Before midnight, the civilian traffic radar at Semmerzake sees the same contact in the same place.

73
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:55,800
A second radar, around a hundred miles away, confirms it.

74
00:04:56,266 --> 00:04:58,175
Two radars, one position.

75
00:04:58,175 --> 00:05:03,138
That is why the Air Force decides this is no longer only a story.

76
00:05:03,138 --> 00:05:06,115
Every condition on its list is now met.

77
00:05:06,115 --> 00:05:09,933
At eleven fifty-six, the order is given: scramble.

78
00:05:10,399 --> 00:05:12,859
But the weather log adds a detail.

79
00:05:12,859 --> 00:05:21,899
Clear sky, light wind, and a minor temperature inversion near three thousand feet, a warm layer of air lying over a cold one.

80
00:05:22,366 --> 00:05:24,604
Inversions bend radar beams.

81
00:05:24,604 --> 00:05:29,879
Radar then returns echoes from layers of air, and from the ground.

82
00:05:29,879 --> 00:05:32,037
Operators call them angels.

83
00:05:32,037 --> 00:05:35,394
This does not prove the blip was an angel.

84
00:05:35,394 --> 00:05:36,433
It is a flag.

85
00:05:36,899 --> 00:05:44,786
Sceptics point to this clear-air effect, called Bragg scattering, as the reason for many of the weaker radar contacts.

86
00:05:44,786 --> 00:05:47,392
Supporters say a controller would know.

87
00:05:47,392 --> 00:05:50,199
The truth is, we cannot test it from here.

88
00:05:50,666 --> 00:05:57,515
So the file now holds three things: lights on the ground, blips on two radars, and a bright question.

89
00:05:57,515 --> 00:05:58,600
Fighters launch.

90
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:00,114
Chapter four.

91
00:06:00,114 --> 00:06:01,162
The scramble.

92
00:06:01,162 --> 00:06:12,933
Five past midnight, two F-16s lift off from Beauvechain air base, call signs AL seventeen and AL twenty-three, guided by Glons toward the contact.

93
00:06:13,399 --> 00:06:17,383
On board, each jet carries its own fire-control radar.

94
00:06:17,383 --> 00:06:22,399
Over the next hour, the pilots will try nine separate interceptions.

95
00:06:22,866 --> 00:06:26,214
At thirteen minutes past midnight, the first lock.

96
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:30,433
Six miles ahead of the nose, nine thousand feet, tracking west.

97
00:06:30,899 --> 00:06:32,379
Then the target moves.

98
00:06:32,379 --> 00:06:40,518
According to the Air Force chronology, in a moment its speed jumps from one hundred and fifty knots to nearly a thousand.

99
00:06:40,518 --> 00:06:45,900
Its height drops, rises to eleven thousand feet, then plunges toward the ground.

100
00:06:46,366 --> 00:06:48,518
The lock breaks within seconds.

101
00:06:48,518 --> 00:06:57,266
Glons, watching on its own screen, tells the pilots something strange: the jets are already flying over the target's position.

102
00:06:57,733 --> 00:07:00,481
Over the next forty minutes, it repeats.

103
00:07:00,481 --> 00:07:07,833
A contact appears, the jets turn, a lock holds for ten to sixty seconds, and the target seems to leap away.

104
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:16,994
One contact is clocked at seven hundred and forty knots, and as it breaks, the pilot's own warning panel shows radar jamming.

105
00:07:16,994 --> 00:07:21,933
Another runs at nearly seven hundred knots toward Bierset and vanishes.

106
00:07:22,399 --> 00:07:26,495
On the ground, the witnesses watch the jets pass overhead.

107
00:07:26,495 --> 00:07:27,342
Three times.

108
00:07:27,342 --> 00:07:30,590
At the third pass, they say the lights change.

109
00:07:30,590 --> 00:07:35,533
One blinks hard, the others vanish, and the brightest dims slowly out.

110
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,069
Now the problem.

111
00:07:37,069 --> 00:07:39,075
The pilots never saw anything.

112
00:07:39,075 --> 00:07:41,415
No lights, no shape, no silhouette.

113
00:07:41,415 --> 00:07:47,366
Their eyes, in a clear sky, found nothing where their radar insisted there was something.

114
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:50,183
The jets land at ten past one.

115
00:07:50,183 --> 00:07:54,571
The last light on the ground goes out around one thirty.

116
00:07:54,571 --> 00:07:57,783
No wreckage, no sound, no physical trace.

117
00:07:57,783 --> 00:07:58,566
Only data.

118
00:07:59,033 --> 00:08:01,177
And how many locks were there?

119
00:08:01,177 --> 00:08:03,892
Here is a detail most retellings skip.

120
00:08:03,892 --> 00:08:06,750
The Air Force summary says six lock-ons.

121
00:08:06,750 --> 00:08:09,966
The detailed Air Force chronology says three.

122
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:17,531
And in three cases both F-16s locked at once, with the same numbers, while flying two miles apart.

123
00:08:17,531 --> 00:08:21,080
To some, that proves two machines saw one object.

124
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,833
To others, there is a simpler reading.

125
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:25,326
Chapter five.

126
00:08:25,326 --> 00:08:26,511
The photograph.

127
00:08:26,511 --> 00:08:34,566
In April nineteen ninety, a few days after the F-16 night, a photo explodes across Belgian newspapers.

128
00:08:35,033 --> 00:08:39,370
A black triangle with a light at each corner, above the trees.

129
00:08:39,370 --> 00:08:43,148
Taken by an anonymous photographer near Petit-Rechain.

130
00:08:43,148 --> 00:08:46,366
It looks exactly like the witnesses described.

131
00:08:46,833 --> 00:08:48,692
Laboratories examine it.

132
00:08:48,692 --> 00:08:55,431
NASA, the French national research centre CNRS, and the Belgian Royal Military Academy.

133
00:08:55,431 --> 00:08:57,600
None can prove it is a fake.

134
00:08:58,066 --> 00:09:02,500
For twenty years, SOBEPS keeps defending the photograph as genuine.

135
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:06,933
Yet sceptics noticed that it could be reproduced with simple props.

136
00:09:07,399 --> 00:09:14,495
Then, in July two thousand and eleven, a man called Patrick Maréchal goes on Belgian television.

137
00:09:14,495 --> 00:09:17,600
He says he made it, to fool his workmates.

138
00:09:18,066 --> 00:09:20,776
A triangle of styrofoam, painted black.

139
00:09:20,776 --> 00:09:22,722
A flashlight in each corner.

140
00:09:22,722 --> 00:09:24,042
Hung from a string.

141
00:09:24,042 --> 00:09:28,975
He still had the photographs from the attempts it took to get it right.

142
00:09:28,975 --> 00:09:32,033
The best-known image of the wave was a hoax.

143
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:33,369
Chapter six.

144
00:09:33,369 --> 00:09:34,022
The file.

145
00:09:34,022 --> 00:09:43,299
July eleventh, nineteen ninety, Colonel Wilfried De Brouwer of the Belgian Air Staff briefs the press on the night of the F-16s.

146
00:09:43,766 --> 00:09:45,667
His conclusion is careful.

147
00:09:45,667 --> 00:09:51,150
The Air Force could not identify the nature or the origin of the phenomena.

148
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:52,758
That sentence is real.

149
00:09:52,758 --> 00:09:56,633
It is the heart of every alien claim about this case.

150
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:58,943
But read what comes next.

151
00:09:58,943 --> 00:10:01,746
The Air Force lists what it ruled out.

152
00:10:01,746 --> 00:10:08,089
Balloons, ultralights, drones, aircraft including stealth, laser projections, mirages.

153
00:10:08,089 --> 00:10:13,399
Each for a reason: speeds, hovering, silence, no clouds to project onto.

154
00:10:13,866 --> 00:10:15,581
Notice the word stealth.

155
00:10:15,581 --> 00:10:19,940
For many, a secret American aircraft was the obvious suspect.

156
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:24,799
The Air Force said no: any aircraft makes noise, and none can hover.

157
00:10:25,266 --> 00:10:28,697
A later chapter of the file is more technical.

158
00:10:28,697 --> 00:10:38,766
Belgian Air Force engineers re-examined the F-16 radar recordings frame by frame, thirty times a second, rebuilding the targets' paths.

159
00:10:39,233 --> 00:10:47,325
Their finding, as reported: the radar's own tracking filter can create jumps, ghost tracks and sudden target swaps.

160
00:10:47,325 --> 00:10:51,899
The wild manoeuvres were partly made by the machine, not the sky.

161
00:10:52,366 --> 00:10:58,383
Auguste Meessen had argued that two radar traces had no conventional explanation.

162
00:10:58,383 --> 00:11:03,732
A cover note on that report says he later withdrew the alien conclusion.

163
00:11:03,732 --> 00:11:07,966
That detail comes second-hand, so we label it unverified.

164
00:11:08,433 --> 00:11:19,466
That report reportedly reached the public only in two thousand and twenty-five, with the radar performance figures blacked out, because the same radar is still in service.

165
00:11:19,933 --> 00:11:20,833
Chapter seven.

166
00:11:20,833 --> 00:11:21,670
After action.

167
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:30,033
The wave is said to have been witnessed by thirteen and a half thousand people, of whom two thousand six hundred wrote statements.

168
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:36,756
Those numbers are real, but they are not a count of people watching the F-16 chase live.

169
00:11:36,756 --> 00:11:40,240
Most reports came after the press asked for them.

170
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:43,866
A report filed late is a memory, not a measurement.

171
00:11:44,333 --> 00:11:48,858
This is the pattern the American sceptic Philip Klass described.

172
00:11:48,858 --> 00:11:50,484
News makes people look.

173
00:11:50,484 --> 00:11:52,181
Looking makes them find.

174
00:11:52,181 --> 00:11:57,766
Finding makes news, until the media lose interest and the wave dies on its own.

175
00:11:58,233 --> 00:12:05,693
Belgian sceptics, among them Marc Hallet, Jean-Michel Abrassart and Pierre Magain, argued exactly that.

176
00:12:05,693 --> 00:12:12,066
Planets, aircraft, helicopters, even laser shows, seen by people already primed to look.

177
00:12:12,533 --> 00:12:15,248
But sceptics also have a hole to fill.

178
00:12:15,248 --> 00:12:21,536
Why do so many police officers describe similar triangles, so close, over so many weeks?

179
00:12:21,536 --> 00:12:24,966
We simply do not have a complete, tested answer.

180
00:12:25,433 --> 00:12:34,427
What we have is a puzzle with a missing piece: no wreckage, no clear photograph, no radar record that survives careful analysis.

181
00:12:34,427 --> 00:12:35,270
Not nothing.

182
00:12:35,270 --> 00:12:37,799
But not what the headlines promised.

183
00:12:38,266 --> 00:12:38,799
Debrief.

184
00:12:38,799 --> 00:12:40,866
Time to score the Belgian wave.

185
00:12:41,333 --> 00:12:45,895
Police and civilians reporting triangle-like lights: evidence.

186
00:12:45,895 --> 00:12:51,120
Thirteen thousand five hundred witnesses: partial, mostly late reports.

187
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:56,419
Radar blips on two ground stations: evidence, still not fully explained.

188
00:12:56,419 --> 00:13:00,466
The F-16 chase proving a craft that outflew jets: weak.

189
00:13:00,933 --> 00:13:04,346
The Petit-Rechain photograph: debunked, a hoax.

190
00:13:04,346 --> 00:13:10,519
Anomalous speeds as real flight: doubtful, since the tracking filter can create them.

191
00:13:10,519 --> 00:13:13,133
Extraterrestrial craft: no evidence.

192
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:14,455
The verdict.

193
00:13:14,455 --> 00:13:16,378
Unexplained, but not alien.

194
00:13:16,378 --> 00:13:21,936
Something odd happened over Belgium: lights, a radar puzzle, honest witnesses.

195
00:13:21,936 --> 00:13:29,133
But the dramatic parts, the supersonic leaps and the perfect photograph, fall apart under inspection.

196
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:32,342
And the human story remains remarkable.

197
00:13:32,342 --> 00:13:39,586
Police, pilots and radar crews did their jobs, wrote it all down, and the Air Force published the file.

198
00:13:39,586 --> 00:13:42,399
That is how a mystery should be handled.

199
00:13:42,866 --> 00:13:49,274
Next case: tiny golden objects from ancient Colombia that look uncannily like jet aircraft.

200
00:13:49,274 --> 00:13:53,500
Are they pre-Columbian aeroplanes, or just a very good fish?

201
00:13:53,966 --> 00:13:56,096
What do you think those lights were?

202
00:13:56,096 --> 00:14:01,066
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
